{
  "provenance": {
    "source": "hand-authored",
    "date": "2026-09-08",
    "evidence": "changes/2026-09-08-router-error-codes.md (ratified 2026-09-08); spec/vocabularies.md § ErrorCode (unknown_model, ambiguous_model) and § Open string namespaces (provider spelling); playbooks/api-family.md § The core loop (the router's three rungs: prefix, catalog, rule). Expected values derived from the reference's lm15.router after the fix and confirmed against the Rust port's lm15-vet; both shims green at authoring. 2026-10-02: rule, rule-boundary and resolve_openai_chat_model cases added by changes/2026-10-02-router-tables-pinned.md; expected values written by hand from DEFAULT_RULES and LITELLM_PROVIDER_PREFIXES (the reference tables every port copies under port.md rule 2), then run through every shim."
  },
  "description": "Model-string resolution fixtures exercised through each implementation's resolve_model op (PROTOCOL.md). The harness supplies the model string and, when the case carries one, an explicit catalog of canonical ModelInfo values; it always supplies an empty env so no real environment leaks in. resolve is pure: no network, no credential lookup. A pass compares Resolution.provider / model / source strictly, or — for an error case — the failure reply's error class, code and the payload fields the vocabulary pins (model; providers for ambiguous_model). Source values: prefix (rung 1), catalog (rung 2), rule (rung 3). A case with \"op\": \"resolve_openai_chat_model\" goes through that op instead (the router's OpenAI-SDK/litellm door: provider/model read through the litellm prefix table, a bare OpenAI name to openai-chat); the comparison is the same.",
